WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.020
I wanted this to be good.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.759
I really did.

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:08.160
But that being said, I think you’ll still
enjoy this episode of Things You Missed.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.759
Stick around to the end of this video to hear
about all the references to other Stephen

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.060
King novels.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.120
[Intro]

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.810
The new 2019 adaptation of Pet Sematary takes
a lot of inspiration from the 1989 version

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.110
of the film -- which is kind of surprising
to me.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:27.039
When we covered the original -- you can watch
that Things You Missed episode right here

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:30.320
-- I talked about the foreshadowing where
Gage is seen crashing red toy truck.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.630
The 2019 Gage offers a similar clue in the
car when the family drives to the house -- he

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.820
smashes a toy truck into the carrying case
that they use to transport their cat Church.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.670
As they arrive in Ludlow, Louis drives through
the “downtown area”, and I’m obviously

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.219
using that term lightly, but this isn’t
something that is in the first movie, and

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:50.780
it’s not really in the book, but it does
resemble the downtown Ludlow seen in Pet Sematary

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:51.780
II.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.710
It may be that both films are basing this
off of a real life location -- but I wasn’t

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.569
able to find one in quick Google Search.

00:00:56.569 --> 00:01:00.219
Louis is seen in a Cape Cod shirt, and the
reason for that is that the Creed family is

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:02.249
now from Boston as opposed to Chicago.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:05.930
That may seem like a popular choice now, but
we’ll see who wins the World Series and

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.500
Super Bowl this year…

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.010
When they first arrive at their new property,
Louis makes an off-hand comment about The

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.200
Great And Terrible.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.320
This line also comes up again when Ellie mentions
it during her killing spree at the end of

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.320
the movie.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.640
But what is The Great And Terrible?

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.220
This is a line from the book that becomes
a motif that is repeated throughout.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.031
The term originates when Rachel is helping
nurse her dying sister Zelda as a child, where

00:01:25.031 --> 00:01:29.290
she pronounces the classic Wizard of Oz character
as “Oz the Gweat and Tewwible”.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.940
It serves as a label for Zelda’s horrible
behaviour towards the family in her final

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.770
days, and it constantly pops into Louis’s
head as a representation of his demons during

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:37.770
the story.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.990
The last section of the book is titled Oz
the Gweat and Tewwible.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.660
Before Louis starts his job at the University
of Maine infirmary he mentions that his previous

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.590
occupation was working the graveyard shift
at the emergency room, a little nod to Stephen

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.040
King’s short story, “Graveyard Shift”,
which also became a movie.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:02:00.540
"This is a new horror from the mind of Stephen
King.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.420
Graveyard.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.420
Shift."

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.420
He also mentions that he had to treat four
bloody noses on his first day.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.149
This phenomenon is further explained in the
book, basically the University students would

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:13.219
come in with minor issues in order to get
out of their exams.

00:02:13.219 --> 00:02:16.440
Back at the house, Rachel and Ellie explore
their new property, when they come across

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.810
the funeral procession of kids wearing animals
masks.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.609
The drum beat played by these children as
they make their way to the Pet Sematary is

00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:25.819
very tribal, and the ritualistic practices
are very reminiscent of a Native American

00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:29.909
practice, like perhaps the burial practices
of the Mic Mac Indians who buried their dead

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:31.879
in the area beyond the Pet Sematary.

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:35.340
Ellie would later go back and check out the
Pet Sematary for herself, and when she does

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.920
there’s a little picture frame in the bottom
right corner with a photo of a parrot, a reference

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.219
to a grave Louis notices in the book for a
pet named Polynesia, who died in 1953.

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:48.109
The headstone for Trixie appears almost exactly
as it is described in the book, right down

00:02:48.109 --> 00:02:51.659
to the misspelled “kilt on the highway”,
and only forgoing the date of death.

00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:55.240
There’s another pet grave marker that King
described in the book who appears in the movie,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.240
and that is Biffer.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.939
In the book, Biffer is a cocker spaniel belonging
to the Desslers’ family, and Biffer was

00:02:59.939 --> 00:03:03.909
run over by a dumpster truck one year before
Louis brings his family to Maine.

00:03:03.909 --> 00:03:08.080
But in this movie, Biffer is Jud Crandall’s
childhood dog who he revived in the Mic Mac

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.999
grounds before finally laying him to rest
in the Pet Sematary.

00:03:10.999 --> 00:03:15.670
Jud’s dog in the book is named Spot, so
I’m not really sure why these were switched…

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.849
much like a lot things in this movie.

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:21.080
One more detail on this scene -- Ellie’s
shirt has a butterfly design on it.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.859
There are a few examples of butterfly imagery
associated with the Ellie.

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:26.829
There’s a picture of a butterfly framed
above the family during dinner and she also

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:29.169
draws a picture of a butterfly and a bee later
on.

00:03:29.169 --> 00:03:32.279
This drawing connects to the scene where Ellie
wears the butterfly shirt, where she gets

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:36.010
stung by a bee while trying to climb the deadfall
that leads to the Mic Mac burial grounds.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.249
But why was she drawn towards the burial grounds?

00:03:38.249 --> 00:03:42.569
The answer comes later on when Jud is explaining
why he showed Louis the burial ground, despite

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:44.260
his own dog coming back evil.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.549
He tells Louis that you just find the “sweetest
smelling reasons to go back to the burial

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:49.549
ground”.

00:03:49.549 --> 00:03:53.340
So if Ellie represents a butterfly, I think
she is attracted to the sweet smelling flower

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.040
that is the Mic Mac grounds.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.520
When Ellie returns from the Pet Sematary,
she has some questions for her father about

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.559
death, which brings up an argument between
Louis and Rachel about how it should be explained

00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:03.559
to their children.

00:04:03.559 --> 00:04:07.609
This triggers a flashback to Rachel’s childhood
where she had to tend to her sister Zelda.

00:04:07.609 --> 00:04:11.970
In the 1989 movie, Zelda’s wardrobe matches
that of this creepy painting seen in the house.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.650
In this 2019 film, she wears the same gown
as the Grady Twins from The Shining.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.590
A patient named Victor Pascow is brought in
to Louis at the University after being struck

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:20.590
by a car.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:24.000
Louis has no chance to save him, and after
his death, the lights flicker as he comes

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.530
back to give Louis a warning.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.800
The flickering lights seem to be a side effect
of the appearance of ghosts, because when

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.330
Pascow comes back later to give Louis a second
warning, the lights flicker in Louis’s basement.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.789
Ellie goes over to bring some cookies to Jud
Crandall, who they still don’t really know

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:40.182
at that point so I’m kind of surprised that
Rachel would have let her go inside of this

00:04:40.182 --> 00:04:41.479
guy's house alone…

00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:46.199
But luckily for Ellie, this is the Pet Sematary
movie, not Romeo Lacoste’s YouTube channel.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:48.560
But there are noteworthy details in Jud’s
house.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.350
He’s a hunter in this movie, he’s even
got the typical deer mount on his wall.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.200
You have to wonder if he became a hunter to
try to get revenge on The Wendigo, the deity

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.849
that revives the corpses buried in the Mic
Mac grounds and possessed his dog Biffer.

00:04:59.849 --> 00:05:03.409
The Wendigo legends tell of a creature with
a stag’s head, so maybe Jud was searching

00:05:03.409 --> 00:05:05.620
for The Wendigo when he acquired this mount.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.740
There are a couple of other references to
The Wendigo.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.920
We hear it’s call at the very beginning
of the movie...

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.680
And as Jud exits the forest for the first
time, you hear the bugs go crazy, I think

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.110
this has something to do with the Wendigo’s
influence.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.860
After burying Church, Jud explains the curse
of the Wendigo to Louis and how he first found

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:30.220
out about it from a boy named Stanny B, and
finally, Louis researches the mythos in this

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.910
book on Northeastern tribes.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:36.240
I did a whole video on the 400 year history
of the Wendigo in Pet Sematary, which you

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.360
can watch here, or just look for the link
in the description at the end of this video.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.600
Jud also tells Ellie about his wife Norma
who got sick and passed away years ago.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.430
Norma is actually a character, who doesn’t
pass away until three months into the novel.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.800
Louis saves her life when she has a heart
attack on Halloween, which is part of the

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.970
reason that Jud feels he has to help Louis
revive his daughter’s cat as kind of a thank

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:55.970
you of sorts.

00:05:55.970 --> 00:06:00.009
But in both movie adaptations now, Norma is
already dead before the story begins.

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:04.259
At least she is mentioned in the 2019 film,
and when Jud faces Ellie’s zombie in the

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.040
final act, she seems to take the form of Norma
momentarily to mess with Jud.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.820
Ellie’s favorite pastime is dancing.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:21.979
She can be seen doing “The Dance of the
Sugar Plum Fairy”, from the ballet The Nutcracker.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:25.449
The Nutcracker is a ballet about a toy Nutcracker
that comes to life as a prince and battles

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:26.720
with the Queen of Mice.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:30.360
In the story, the family’s cat is brought
back to life and acquires a habit of killing

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.220
small animals like mice and birds, although,
the only example I think we see in this film,

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.860
is Church bringing home the dead bird.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.830
Victor Pascow tries to give another warning
to Louis by taking him to the Pet Sematary

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.360
and showing him the barrier that should not
be broken.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.530
At the end of this sequence, a hand comes
out of the ground and grabs him.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:49.320
This could be a reference to an iconic scene
from Carrie, or at the very least, a clue

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.920
to Louis that going to the land beyond the
Pet Sematary could put him in danger of being

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.320
attacked by zombies.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.610
The next major event comes on Halloween.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.710
Ellie is dressed as a witch, and she jokingly
threatens to put a spell on Jud, to which

00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:02.729
he replies you already have.

00:07:02.729 --> 00:07:06.280
This could be a subtle reference to the fact
that The Wendigo already casted an influence

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.970
on Jud all those years ago when he buried
his dog.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:13.229
In fact, later that night, when they go to
bury Church, Jud takes Louis to the Pet Sematary,

00:07:13.229 --> 00:07:17.280
and unlike the book and the original movie,
it looks like Jud’s original intention is

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.919
to let Louis bury Church in the Pet Sematary.

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:23.539
But if you watch the scene, there’s a noticeable
moment where something gets Jud’s attention

00:07:23.539 --> 00:07:26.750
and he stares off at the deadfall in the direction
of the cursed burial ground.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:30.710
It’s like I mentioned earlier, the Wendigo
traps him with the sweet smell of the burial

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:33.270
ground and there’s nothing he can do to
resist it.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:37.060
Ellie goes trick-or-treating with two other
kids dressed as ghosts, which may represent

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:40.820
Pascow and Church, the two deaths that have
occured up to that point.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:46.160
“That’s the cutest ****ing thing I’ve
ever seen in my entire life.”

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.780
“UH-HEH!!”

00:07:47.789 --> 00:07:51.689
When Jud tells Louis about his cat being killed,
he mentions it was “one of those Orinco

00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:52.689
trucks.”

00:07:52.689 --> 00:07:55.930
Orinco is a truck company most prominently
seen in the Pet Sematary novel, where they

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.610
run down Church and Gage, and they also make
smaller appearances in some other Stephen

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:00.610
King works.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:04.330
When they are going to bury Church, Louis
notices this spiral image on the trees.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:08.169
When they are later researching the burial
ground, they see more imagery of this spiral.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:09.169
What does this mean?

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:12.520
In King’s novel, the spiral serves as a
symbol of the bridge between the world of

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:14.130
the living and the gulf.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.509
When Louis visits Gage’s grave one night,
he finds himself thinking about the layout

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:21.110
of the Pet Sematary, and how it mimics the
ancient religious religious symbol seen on

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:26.229
the tombs of Pharaohs, the barrows of Venetian
kings, cave walls, stonehenge and Bibles.

00:08:26.229 --> 00:08:29.639
Then he shocks himself by looking down and
realizing that he has drawn the spiral with

00:08:29.639 --> 00:08:31.220
his his finger in the dirt subconsciously.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.719
When he goes back to the burial ground to
re-bury Gage, he notices that the rock cairns

00:08:35.719 --> 00:08:40.370
knocked over by the revived corpses escaping
their tombs come together to form a gigantic

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.060
spiral when seen from above.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.190
So Louis and Jud successfully bury Church,
and the next night, Church returns from the

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:47.889
dead and goes back to Ellie’s room.

00:08:47.889 --> 00:08:51.019
Rachel tells Louis, “good thing you’re
not a vet,” which is another little nod

00:08:51.019 --> 00:08:55.770
to the 1991 film Pet Sematary II, which is
not based off any Stephen King writing, but

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.829
features a main character who is a vet.

00:08:57.829 --> 00:09:01.290
Louis goes to confront Jud who explains everything
about the Wendigo.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.519
They look at a map where you can spot that
they are in close proximity to Little God

00:09:04.519 --> 00:09:08.661
Swamp, the area in between the Pet Sematary
and Mic Mac burial ground in the book where

00:09:08.661 --> 00:09:10.770
Louis has his encounter with the Wendigo.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.829
The Wendigo isn’t shown in either movie,
but there is a moment where he hears a branch

00:09:14.829 --> 00:09:18.800
snap and looks into the distance -- where,
based on his reaction, it is implied that

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:19.800
something moved.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.019
He also looks up old headlines from the Ludlow
Tribune that report on the incident with Hanratty

00:09:24.019 --> 00:09:25.019
the Bull.

00:09:25.019 --> 00:09:28.430
This is another small easter egg pointing
to events in the novel, where a man named

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:32.840
Zack McGovern tried to revive his prized bull
using the Mic Mac burial ground, and the bull

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.290
came back as one of the most evil creatures
ever to be revived.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:40.300
It would charge at small animals, people and
even trees swaying in the breeze.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:45.380
It caused a ton of damage around town, nearly
destroying itself before being put back down.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:49.550
There’s also a brief glimpse at a headline
about Timmy Baterman, the son of Bill Baterman.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:53.330
Timmy died in World War II and his grief stricken
father tried to bring him back by burying

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:54.700
him in the Indian burial ground.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:58.140
If you’ve seen the original movie, you know
a bit more about this story, as it’s only

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.110
mentioned in passing in the new one.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:04.010
So with Church being back, Ellie tries to
comb his fur, but she has a hard time, exclaiming,

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:05.630
“Church, you’re so sticky!

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:06.730
There’s something gross!”

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.860
Well you shouldn’t have let Shane Dawson
babysit your cat should you?

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:13.780
But in all seriousness, this serves as a setup
for when Louis tries to comb Ellie’s hair

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:17.740
in the bath after her death and discovers
the staples still in the back of her head,

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.670
that were used to hold her together for the
funeral.

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:23.100
But before we get to that, I want to highlight
the scene where Louis notices Ellie drawing

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.100
pictures.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.990
I already mentioned the significance of the
butterfly and bee drawing, but there are two

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.420
other drawings that may be significant.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.120
Ellie’s other drawing is a picture of what
appears to be a whale in front of a rainbow.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.680
Now this one, I admit this could be a stretch, but I’ll just say it and let

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.860
you decide how intentional it actually was.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.200
So the zombie version of Ellie would later
use the form of Zelda as a psychological attack

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:55.620
on Rachel and The Legend of Zelda video game
franchise has three flying Leviathan creatures

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:59.110
that resemble whales known as Levias, The
Wind Fish and Oshus.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:03.230
Again, I’m not saying this was an intentional
connection, but it seems like a strange thing

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:04.830
for a child to draw.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.470
The other picture is drawn by Gage, and it
appears as a disturbing image of a monster,

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:10.710
which is implied to be a warning about The
Wendigo.

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:14.260
I did a video where I talked about how Ellie
uses The Shine to try to warn her father about

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:15.660
the dangers that await them.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:19.500
But since Ellie and Gage’s roles are switched
for some reason in this movie, I think this

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:21.890
confirms that Gage is now the resident psychic.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:25.990
He is also the one who later has the dream
about Pascow that later sends Rachel rushing

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:30.110
back to Maine to try and stop Louis from re-burying
their daughter -- so if you thought Rachel

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:34.490
taking a cross country flight based on the
dream of a child was a bit much, in this movie

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:36.720
she’s going back based on the dream of a
2 year old.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.470
Maybe that’s why they switched Rachel’s
family being from Chicago to Boston in this

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.820
one, it’s a bit of shorter trip and perhaps
more believable.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:47.130
But before we get to that part, there’s
a scare with Church possibly attacking Gage

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:51.200
in his crib, which leads Louis to release
the cat just before Ellie’s 9th birthday

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.200
party.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.079
They invite some of their friends from Boston
to this party, and one of them can be overheard

00:11:55.079 --> 00:11:58.740
in the background muttering something about
a big St. Bernard with rabies.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:02.910
This is a reference to another Stephen King
book, Cujo, about a St. Bernard dog who gets

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.500
bit by a bat and turns into a dangerous beast.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:10.089
This reference also exists in different forms
in both the novel and 80s movie, but there’s

00:12:10.089 --> 00:12:14.110
also a direct reference to the 80s movie itself;
in that one the truck driver is listening

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:18.560
to a song called “Sheena is a Punk Rocker”
by The Ramones when he runs over Gage.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.760
"Sheena is.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.100
A punk rocker.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.160
Sheena is.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.740
A punk rocker!"

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:28.960
In the new movie, he gets distracted by a
phone call from a contact named Sheena right

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:30.200
before he hits Ellie.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:34.080
Ellie goes into the coffin wearing white,
as a symbol for her purity and child-like

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.080
innocence.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:38.450
When she later returns as a zombie, she changes
into the dirty clothes that she wore during

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:42.399
the accident, signifying that she’s now
tainted by the evil influence of the Wendigo.

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:46.430
When Louis returns from relocating his daughter’s
burial place to the Mic Mac grounds, I spotted

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:49.200
a height measurement chart for the kids in
the Creed’s house.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.420
This might be shown to make us think of the
heights Ellie will never reach having met

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:55.770
an early death, or it could be about the fact
that Louis had to dig her up.

00:12:55.770 --> 00:13:00.570
The maximum height on the chart goes up to
6ft... actually, six feet isn't down there,

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:02.100
it's more like, right here.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:05.279
But 6 feet is typically the depth at which
coffins are buried.

00:13:05.279 --> 00:13:08.959
When Louis wakes up the next day he hears
Ellie once again dancing to the Sugar Plum

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:13.500
Fairy song from the Nutcracker, but this time
her gracefulness is gone, and she’s wrecking

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.540
the house and just basically being a brat.

00:13:19.089 --> 00:13:23.860
Meanwhile, the rest of the family is staying
with Rachel’s dad in Boston.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:27.320
As I mentioned earlier, Gage has the Shine
and senses something is wrong.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:31.160
A lot of characters throughout Stephen King’s
entire body of work have “shining” moments,

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.759
but this one is particularly emphasized by
the fact that Gage wakes up from his nap and

00:13:34.759 --> 00:13:39.330
starts screaming “Pascow” over and over
in the same manner that Danny yells out “Redrum”

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.579
in the Shining.

00:13:40.579 --> 00:13:41.709
"Danny?"

00:13:41.709 --> 00:13:42.839
"Redrum!

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:43.970
Redrum!

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:45.460
Redrum!

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:47.560
Redrum!"

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.420
"Danny, what's the matter honey?"

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:50.430
"Redrum!"

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:52.269
"You having a bad dream?"

00:13:52.269 --> 00:13:55.740
So this dream causes Rachel to drive back
to Maine with Gage, where she gets stuck in

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:57.050
traffic on the highway.

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:00.740
On the side of the road, there’s a sign
saying they are 20 miles from the exit for

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:04.380
Derry, which is a fictional town in Stephen
King’s books, most notably, the setting

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.430
of IT, Insomnia and Dreamcatcher.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:09.270
The rest of the movie plays out very similarly
to the original.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:13.910
Ellie brings a scalpel to a gun fight and
somehow wins, then returns to her own house

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:14.910
to try to kill Rachel.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.470
At this point in the movie, you can spot a
painting of Emerald City from the Wizard of

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.569
Oz, another low-key reference to Oz the Gweat
and Tewwible.

00:14:21.569 --> 00:14:25.279
It makes sense to see the painting at this
point in the movie because Oz represents the

00:14:25.279 --> 00:14:28.449
evil entities that all the characters are
starting to become.

00:14:28.449 --> 00:14:32.170
When Rachel makes it home and sees that Louis
has used this cursed magic to bring back their

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:36.259
daughter, she disagrees with the decision
because she realizes that it’s not natural.

00:14:36.259 --> 00:14:38.270
Louis has his own reasoning for doing it though.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.470
He argues, “Let God take his own kid.”

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.880
This is a reference to something Ellie says
near the beginning of the book after first

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:46.550
discovering the Pet Sematary and learning
about death.

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:49.780
“He’s not God's cat he's my cat!

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:52.940
Let God have his own if he wants one.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:54.500
Not mine...”

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:58.820
The movie ends with Louis getting impaled
by a cross and Gage being the only one left,

00:14:58.839 --> 00:15:02.500
with his three zombie family members (four
if we count Church) coming for him.

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:06.029
I think it’s implied that he doesn’t survive,
which to me… alright let me just put my

00:15:06.029 --> 00:15:10.220
opinion just this once… is less scary than
letting the remaining child actually survive,

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:13.870
because when Ellie survives in the original,
she’s gonna be stuck with all of those scarring

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:18.509
memories and also living with her ****bag
grandparents, which is definitely not a plus.

00:15:18.509 --> 00:15:21.540
"You killer of children!"

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:22.540
"Daddy!!"

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:26.190
So the credits roll, but there’s one more
Thing that You (might have) Missed.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:30.300
Actually, I nearly did miss this, because
they turned off the audio to begin a Q&amp;A with

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:34.290
the cast and directors at my screening, but
somehow I noticed in the credits, a cover

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:38.360
of Pet Sematary by the Ramones from a band
called Starcrawler, which is cool because

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:42.190
it’s the same end credit music that Mary
Lambert had in her adaptation of Pet Sematary,

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:46.060
but also kind of defeats the purpose, because
the whole reason she got the Ramones, was

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:49.529
as a reference to Blitzkrieg Bop, the most
well known and probably most expensive to

00:15:49.529 --> 00:15:52.779
license Ramone’s song, which stuck in Louis’s
head throughout the entire novel.

00:15:52.779 --> 00:15:56.180
That’s one of the many things I talked about
in my Things You Missed episode on the original

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:59.589
Pet Sematary film, so click the video on the
left if you want to see that.

00:15:59.589 --> 00:16:04.370
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:07.390
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:12.529
Assuming we both get buried, in a Pet Sematary.

